  "plausibility_discussion": "Top reversers are dominated by compounds that repress the 4 detectable injury-elevated genes (HMOX1/FTH1/ACSL4/PTGS2); those are broadly stress/NRF2-responsive so many unrelated compounds score. Mechanistic caveat flagged for the reviewer: HMOX1 and FTH1 are NRF2-driven CYTOPROTECTIVE genes, so 'reversing' (repressing) them is biologically ambiguous -- an NRF2 ACTIVATOR (e.g. sulforaphane, bardoxolone) legitimately INCREASES HMOX1/FTH1 and therefore appears as a MIMICKER, not a reverser, under naive direction matching. Classic ferroptosis INDUCERS (erastin/RSL3) raise stress genes and correctly appear as mimickers. The most defensible hits are those that RESTORE the lost genes (res>0: up-regulate GPX4/SLC7A11), shown in green.",
  "honest_label": "direction-aware reversal connectivity from real LINCS L1000 Chem-Pert consensus signatures (Enrichr library) -- NOT a rank-based CMap/SigCom tau.",
  "no_fabrication": "Every score is computed from the downloaded signature gene sets; no connectivity value is invented."
